## Part 3: 15 interview questions about this project

**1. Walk me through the project in one minute.**
Two systems disagree: order items say what was sold, payments say what was paid. I loaded {{ dataset.raw_rows_total | int }} raw rows from {{ dataset.tables_loaded | int }} tables into DuckDB, ran {{ data_quality.checks_run | int }} data quality checks without deleting anything, and reconciled {{ reconciliation.orders_total | int }} orders. {{ reconciliation.match_rate_pct | pct }} match to the cent. I investigated the {{ reconciliation.orders_unmatched | int }} that don't, found evidence-based causes for all but {{ reconciliation.orders_unexplained | int }}, and the big one is {{ reconciliation.missing_items.paid_brl | brl }} paid on orders with no items. Output: star schema for Power BI, Excel, a dashboard, and a one-page memo.

**2. How did you define a match?**
Paid minus sold within R$ {{ match_tolerance_brl | num2 }}, per order, with sold = item price + freight and paid = sum of payments. Money stored as DECIMAL so cents are exact.

**3. How did you decide the mismatch categories?**
I looked first. Overpaid card orders had paid/sold ratios sitting on fixed values that rise with installments (median {{ reconciliation.interest.median_gap_pct_2x | pct }} over at 2 installments, {{ reconciliation.interest.median_gap_pct_12x | pct }} at 12). That is an interest table. Underpaid orders were short by exactly 5% or 10% of the price, which is a discount. Small gaps were all on multi-item orders, which is rounding. Each rule has a CSV of evidence behind it.

**4. What was the biggest finding?**
{{ reconciliation.missing_items.orders | int }} orders have payments but no items: {{ reconciliation.missing_items.paid_brl | brl }}, {{ reconciliation.by_category.missing_items.share_of_unmatched_gap_pct? | pct }} of all unmatched money. {{ reconciliation.missing_items.canceled_or_unavailable_pct | pct }} are canceled or unavailable, so the question for finance is whether those customers were refunded.

**5. How do you know you did not lose any rows?**
Tests. For every table, input rows = usable rows + quarantined rows, and every order_id from any source appears exactly once in the reconciliation. Those tests run on clean data and again after injecting duplicates, orphans, negative prices and bad types.

**6. How did you test the checks?**
Failure injection. Each test copies a small real fixture, plants one known bad record (duplicate order, orphan payment, negative price, delivered before purchase, payment off by R$ 37) and asserts the exact check and severity fire. I also broke a check on purpose to confirm the tests fail.

**7. Why not delete bad data?**
Deleting changes totals with no audit trail. I route every failing row to an exceptions table with a reason and severity; the clean views exclude high/medium rows, but the raw and staged data are untouched.

**8. Give an example of a data quality issue you found.**
{{ exceptions.by_check_name.carrier_before_purchase? | int }} orders were handed to the carrier before they were purchased, which is impossible, so high severity. {{ exceptions.by_check_name.duplicate_key? | int }} review rows reuse a review id across orders, medium. {{ exceptions.by_check_name.carrier_before_approval? | int }} orders shipped before payment approval was recorded; that is plausible, so low.

**9. Show me a window function you used and why.**
`DENSE_RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY purchase_month ORDER BY abs_gap DESC)` to rank the largest gaps each month, and a running `SUM() OVER (ORDER BY abs_gap DESC)` divided by the total to get a Pareto curve: {{ reconciliation.pareto.orders_for_80pct_of_gap | int }} orders ({{ reconciliation.pareto.pct_of_unmatched_orders | pct1 }} of unmatched) hold 80% of the unmatched money. That tells the team where to start.

**10. How did you avoid double counting when joining items and payments?**
Aggregate each side to one row per order in its own CTE, then join. Joining raw rows would multiply items by payments.

**11. Tell me about the forecast.**
Monthly orders, holdout of the last {{ forecast.horizon_months | int }} complete months, four candidates. {{ forecast.verdict }} {{ forecast.winner | code }} had {{ forecast.winner_mape_pct | pct }} MAPE; the best model had {{ forecast.best_model_mape_pct | pct }}. In the holdout table, Holt's trend predictions run above the actuals because volume flattened in 2018, and seasonal naive copies 2017, a ramp-up year. Whatever wins is what ships, and the docs say which kind won.

**12. What would you do differently with more time?**
Pull refund data to close the missing-items question, get a second year for seasonality and a Black Friday flag (November 2017 was {{ forecast.nov_2017_uplift_vs_oct_pct | pct1 }} above October), move the SQL into dbt with tests, and schedule a daily run with alerts.

**13. Is there a customer impact?**
Yes. Unmatched orders average {{ kpis.reviews_by_match.unmatched.avg_review_score | stars }} stars vs {{ kpis.reviews_by_match.matched.avg_review_score | stars }} for matched orders, and {{ kpis.reviews_by_match.unmatched.pct_reviews_1_or_2 | pct1 }} of their reviews are 1 or 2 stars. Late deliveries show the same pattern: {{ kpis.delivery.avg_review_late | stars }} vs {{ kpis.delivery.avg_review_on_time | stars }}. Correlation, not proof of cause: the missing-items orders are mostly cancellations, and customers rate cancellations badly.

**14. How would a manager use this?**
The memo gives three actions. The Excel has the KPI sheet with definitions and the full list of unmatched orders ranked by money, so a finance analyst can work top-down. The Power BI spec rebuilds the dashboard on the star schema with measures that must equal metrics.json.

**15. What are the limits of your conclusions?**
No refund or bank settlement data, so "missing items" cannot be closed here. Buckets are inferred from strong patterns, not confirmed by Olist. The interest rule catches some orders whose ratio is not on the shared table. The forecast has {{ forecast.train_months | int }} training months.

